# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# SLC Riser script - version v62
__title__ = "SLC\nRiser v62"
__doc__ = ("Draws a one-line SLC riser diagram in a new drafting view named "
           "<LEVEL> - SLC RISER - <PANEL #> (the panel's level and its Panel # parameter, in capitals).\n\n"
           "If a new circuit (its text and callouts included) would overlap the circuit below or above it, the "
           "panel's Loop Spacing is raised to make room and the circuits above move up with it. Loop Spacing 1 is never left below 10'.\n\n"
           "If no conduit is connected to the panel, it is traced from the nearest element within 5' that has conduit "
           "connected - a GUTTER-BOX family 1-2' above the panel first.\n\n"
           "Every one-line ends with 'SLC RETURN'. The panel detail's Panel Model is the panel's Model.\n\n"
           "When you pick the first device, only devices on its loop are drawn: devices of other loops on shared "
           "conduit are passed through.\n\n"
           "Run it from a drafting view instead to get the drafting view options: Update Selected One-Line - "
           "select a one-line group and its circuit is traced again in the model and redrawn in place (same "
           "group name, locked to the same reference line) with the current symbols, callouts and Riser - Config "
           "settings; the panel detail's Panel #, Location and Panel Model are refreshed too. Hand edits inside "
           "the group are replaced. One-lines drawn before v47 are worked out from their group name (Panel #) and "
           "the addresses written in them.\n\n"
           "Update Panel - every one-line of one panel in the view (the only panel there, else chosen from a list) is updated the same way, lowest loop first, as one undo step with one summary at the end; a question (devices with no conduit path, a type without a symbol) is asked once for all of them, and cancelling one leaves the view as it was. Circuits of the panel that aren't drawn in the view yet are not added.\n\n"
           "Steps: choose the head end panel (FACU, NCP, APS ...) from a list - panels with FACU / FACP / FAP / FAC "
           "in the family name that are also set to Head End in Family Defaults Config or have a Panel # parameter - "
           "or select it in the model, then the first device in the circuit "
           "(either can be picked by its tag), choose the conduit types to trace, then check the device "
           "count and adjust spacing / wrapping, then choose to generate a new drafting view (with the panel detail) or add the circuit to "
           "an existing one (joined to the panel detail already there; no new panel is placed) before the riser is drawn. "
           "Each loop's one-line is made into a detail group named <PANEL #> - Loop <letter>. The circuit is followed along "
           "its conduit starting at the first device, away from the panel, so that device decides the "
           "order. Press Esc instead of picking a first device to follow every conduit run leaving the "
           "panel. The tool draws every Fire Alarm Device that has an SLC address (SLC Address, SLC Address 2 ... 24) "
           "in order of connection, one row per conduit run leaving the panel, with its full address "
           "(N#L#M# / N#L#D#) underneath; a device with several addresses shows the first and last on two "
           "lines (first- / last). The address text is at 45 degrees, right justified, hanging below "
           "the riser line, its top corner 1\" under its device; devices are 6' apart, and the riser line runs unbroken through the devices. Each row is labeled with its loop as a letter (L1 = A, L2 = B ...), read from those addresses. "
           "The head end panel is drawn with the panel detail family set on the SLC Riser tab of Riser - Config "
           "(NEX2-DETAIL-RISER by default) (Panel # and Location - the "
           "room name and number - filled in from the panel); every run comes out of its right side, the first "
           "device one device spacing away from the right end of its reference line. The height of loop N is "
           "read from the placed panel's Loop Spacing parameters: each is measured from the line below it (Loop "
           "Spacing 1 from the bottom of the panel), so loop N is Loop Spacing 1 + ... + N above the bottom. "
           "Each loop leaves the panel in line with the family's reference line "
           "(reference line or reference plane) named for that loop number - 3, L3, Loop 3 or Ref Line 3 in its subcategory, "
           "line style or name (N1L3 -> 3). With no match you choose one of the family's reference lines or "
           "pick a start point. The first line of the one-line is locked in alignment with its reference line. "
           "When a row wraps, the next line runs the opposite way (left to right, then right to left, and so "
           "on), with the devices following it.\n\n"
           "Each loop is one line, with no T-tap rows. A conduit teeing off the main run is treated as a feed "
           "and a return in one conduit: its devices are drawn at the tee, out to the end of the branch and back, "
           "then the main run (the side with the most devices) carries on. Devices on the loop (same "
           "N#L#) that no conduit reaches: you are asked whether to add them in address order, add them at the end of the line, or leave them off.\n\n"
           "Only conduit of the types you choose is followed (the types connected to the first devices are "
           "ticked to start with), so conduit of another type (NAC, power) joined by a tee is ignored.\n\n"
           "A type can have several symbols, drawn in order, one address each (the last symbol takes the rest). "
           "Addresses are 45 degree text under the device, unless the type's Address Group (Family Defaults "
           "Config) has a callout set for the device's subgroup (by its number of addresses) on the SLC Riser tab "
           "of Riser - Config: then that callout symbol is placed instead, in a band below the bottom of the line's address "
           "text, joined to the device by detail lines (down, across, down) and moved right if it would overlap the "
           "callout before it, with its parameters filled from the device's "
           "parameters with the same names (SLC Address ... SLC Address 24, Panel #, ...; names compared with case ignored, the type's value used when the device's own is blank). A device's Panel # still shows above its symbol too. "
           "Each Family : Type is drawn with the symbol(s) set for it on the SLC Riser tab of Riser - Config. A type without "
           "one is asked about when the tool runs, and the choice is saved to the table. "
           "A device whose Panel # parameter has a value shows it above its symbol (5/64\" text).\n\n"
           "View scale, device spacing, devices per line, wrap rounding and the label offset are set on the SLC Riser tab of Riser - Config.\n\n"
           "Devices on the conduit without an SLC address are passed through and listed afterwards.")

import riser_tool        # the riser engine, shared by the risers (lib/riser_tool.py)
import slc_riser         # the SLC loop kind (lib/slc_riser.py)

riser_tool.run(slc_riser.LoopKind())
